ouphavanh"},"Q57826":{"id":"Q57826","name":"Thongsing Thammavong","desc":"Prime Minister of Laos","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Thongsing_Thammavong.jpg/330px-Thongsing_Thammavong.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1944-04-12","died":null,"birthplace":"Hūaphan","parties":["Lao People's Revolutionary Party"],"education":[],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Thongsing Thammavong"},"Q58323":{"id":"Q58323","name":"Thongloun Sisoulith","desc":"General Secretary of the Lao People's Revolutionary Party","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Thongloun_Sisoulith_in_Kazan_-_2024_(cropped_2).jpg/330px-Thongloun_Sisoulith_in_Kazan_-_2024_(cropped_2).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1945-11-10","died":null,"birthplace":"Hūaphan","parties":["Lao People's Revolutionary Party"],"education":["Herzen University","Academy of Social Sciences of the Central Committee of CPSU"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Thongloun Sisoulith"},"Q7180477":{"id":"Q7180477","name":"Phankham Viphavanh","desc":"Laotian politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Phankham_Viphavanh_on_December_14%2C_2022_(cropped).jpg/330px-Phankham_Viphavanh_on_December_14%2C_2022_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1951-04-14","died":null,"birthplace":"French Protectorate of Laos","parties":["Lao People's Revolutionary Party"],"education":[],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Phankham Viphavanh"},"Q104914585":{"id":"Q104914585","name":"Sonexay Siphandone","desc":"Laotian politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Sonexay_Siphandone_10_June_2026.jpg/330px-Sonexay_Siphandone_10_June_2026.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1966-01-26","died":null,"birthplace":null,"parties":[],"education":[],"occupations":["civil servant"],"wiki":"Sonexay Siphandone"}},"summary":"Laos, officially the Lao People's Democratic Republic (LPDR), is a country in Mainland Southeast Asia; it is the only landlocked country in Southeast Asia. It is bordered by Myanmar and China to the northwest, Vietnam to the east, Cambodia to the southeast, and Thailand to the west and southwest. The 2025 population and housing census enumerated 6,744,701 residents. A post-enumeration survey estimated the true resident population at approximately 7.24 million. Its capital and most populous city is Vientiane. It has three UNESCO World Heritage Sites: the town of Luang Prabang, the temple complex of Vat Phou, and the Plain of Jars. Laos participates in the Greater Mekong Subregion economic cooperation programme and is a member of ASEAN, the Asia-Pacific Trade Agreement, East Asia Summit, La Francophonie, and the World Trade Organization.\nThe kingdom of Lan Xang existed from the 14th to 18th centuries and was a hub for overland trade through its territory. In 1707 it split into three kingdoms: Luang Prabang, Vientiane, and Champasak. In 1893, these kingdoms were unified under French rule as part of French Indochina. Laos was under Japanese administration during World War II, gaining independence in 1945 before returning to French administration until achieving autonomy in 1949. It regained full independence in 1953 as the Kingdom of Laos, with a constitutional monarchy under Sisavang Vong. A civil war from 1959 to 1975 saw the communist Pathet Lao (supported by China, North Vietnam, and the Soviet Union) oppose the Royal Lao Armed Forces (backed by the United States). It ended with the Lao People's Revolutionary Party's establishment of the Lao People's Democratic Republic in 1975.","history":"Evidence of modern human presence in the northern and central highlands of Indochina, which constitute the territories of what later is Laos, dates back to the Lower Paleolithic. These earliest human migrants are Austronesians—associated with the Hoabinhian culture—and have populated the highlands and the interior, less accessible regions of Laos and all of Southeast Asia. The subsequent Austroasiatic and Austronesian marine migration waves affected landlocked Laos, and direct Chinese and Indian cultural contact had a greater impact on the country.\nLaos exists in truncated form from the thirteenth-century Lao kingdom of Lan Xang, which existed as a unified kingdom from 1357 to 1707, divided into the three rival kingdoms of Luang Prabang, Vientiane, and Champasak, from 1707 to 1779. It fell to Siamese suzerainty from 1779 to 1893 and was reunified under the French Protectorate of Laos in 1893. The borders of Laos were established by the French colonial government in the 19th and 20th centuries.","historyTitle":"History of Laos","curated":null,"indicators":{"population":[[1960,2121510],[1961,2169800],[1962,2219910],[1963,2271060],[1964,2322830],[1965,2376200],[1966,2431080],[1967,2487720],[1968,2546380],[1969,2606960],[1970,2669700],[1971,2734630],[1972,2801660],[1973,2870570],[1974,2939760],[1975,3005520],[1976,3064880],[1977,3118920],[1978,3171740],[1979,3228200],[1980,3292570],[1981,3366630],[1982,3450030],[1983,3541000],[1984,3637840],[1985,3739990],[1986,3846720],[1987,3957610],[1988,4072600],[1989,4190970],[1990,4311960],[1991,4435120],[1992,4559650],[1993,4685060],[1994,4809220],[1995,4929410],[1996,5045680],[1997,5152010],[1998,5248270],[1999,5340780],[2000,5431210],[2001,5521480],[2002,5610890],[2003,5697950],[2004,5781630],[2005,5869520],[2006,5963200],[2007,6056240],[2008,6148970],[2009,6241640],[2010,6334190],[2011,6426590],[2012,6518980],[2013,6611390],[2014,6703170],[2015,6801650],[2016,6908800],[2017,7018150],[2018,7128050],[2019,7237640],[2020,7346530],[2021,7453190],[2022,7559010],[2023,7664990],[2024,7769820],[2025,7873050]],"gdp":[[1984,1757140000],[1985,2366670000],[1986,1776840000],[1987,1087270000],[1988,598961000],[1989,714047000],[1990,865560000],[1991,1028090000],[1992,1127810000],[1993,1327750000],[1994,1543610000],[1995,1763540000],[1996,1873670000],[1997,1747010000],[1998,1280180000],[1999,1454430000],[2000,1731200000],[2001,1768620000],[2002,1758180000],[2003,2